# Density Forces Pattern

Begin with the central question: how large can a finite structure become while avoiding one chosen pattern? This guided tour moves from an exact graph threshold to an asymptotic theorem about the integers. For background, see [extremal graph theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Extremal_graph_theory), [set-family shadows](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kruskal%E2%80%93Katona_theorem), and [arithmetic progressions](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arithmetic_progression).

## 1. Exact clique avoidance

Use the calculator to divide a network into balanced independent groups. It displays both the exact integer threshold and the smoother quadratic upper bound.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

{{visualization:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the exact theorem and why balancing wins</summary>

For $r\ge2$, write $s=n mod(r-1)$. The greatest number of edges in an $n$-vertex graph without $K_r$ is

$$ rac{(n^2-s^2)(r-2)}{2(r-1)}+ inom{s}{2}.$$

Symmetrization turns an extremizer into a complete multipartite graph. Moving a vertex from a part at least two larger than another part increases the cross-edge count, so the parts must be balanced.

</details>

{{algorithm:0}}

{{demo:0}}

## 2. Boundary is unavoidable

A collection of $r$-sets casts a lower shadow consisting of all its $(r-1)$-subsets. Colexicographic initial segments reuse lower faces as efficiently as possible.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{visualization:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal Kruskal–Katona and the Lovász consequence</summary>

If an $r$-uniform family has at least $ inom{k}{r}$ members, then its $i$-fold lower shadow has at least $ inom{k}{r-i}$ members. Compressions preserve family size while never enlarging the shadow; iteration gives every lower rank.

</details>

{{algorithm:1}}

{{demo:1}}

## 3. From huge graphs to bounded maps

[Szemerédi’s regularity lemma](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Szemer%C3%A9di_regularity_lemma) partitions every finite graph into a bounded number of almost-random-looking pairs. The number of parts depends on the requested accuracy, not on graph order. This compression supports the triangle removal principle: few triangles imply that few edge deletions remove them all.

<details><summary>Reveal the energy and removal argument</summary>

The mean squared inter-part density is bounded by $1$. Every refinement forced by many irregular pairs raises this energy by a fixed positive amount, so refinement terminates. A triangle among dense regular pairs expands into many actual triangles. Therefore a graph with fewer than $\delta n^3$ triangles can be made triangle-free by deleting fewer than $arepsilon n^2$ edges.

</details>

## 4. Equally spaced integers

The arithmetic equation $a+c=2b$ defines a three-term progression. Experiment with finite sets, then compare their small exact extremal densities.

