{{visualization:2}}

<details><summary>Reveal Roth’s theorem and the graph-to-arithmetic bridge</summary>

For every $arepsilon>0$, every sufficiently large subset $A\subseteq\{0,\ldots,N-1\}$ with $|A|\gearepsilon N$ contains $a,b,c\in A$ with $a+c=2b$ and $a
e b$. Equivalently, the largest progression-free size $R(N)$ satisfies $R(N)=o(N)$. Corners in a two-dimensional encoding become triangles in an auxiliary graph; removal forces a nontrivial corner, which decodes to the progression.

</details>

{{algorithm:2}}

{{demo:2}}

## 5. The unifying lesson

Exact optimization, compression, regular approximation, and removal all express one principle: positive density creates many overlapping opportunities for a forbidden pattern, and beyond the extremal threshold at least one opportunity must become real. Continue with the [triangle removal lemma](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Graph_removal_lemma) or explore the research directions on stability, induced removal, quantitative bounds, and four-term progressions.

